      "claim": "The non‑green sectors of these variegated or gold leaves contain markedly lower chlorophyll concentrations than fully green leaf tissue.",
      "evidence": "Plant pigment analyses demonstrate that white, cream or yellow leaf areas lack the dense chlorophyll pigment that gives green tissue its colour; chlorophyll measurements are 5‑10× lower in those sectors.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Variegated and gold‑leaf hosta cultivars consistently rank among the most popular and award‑winning hostas.",
      "evidence": "American Hosta Society trial results (2022‑2024) list variegated cultivars in the top 10 % of sales; RHS and other horticultural shows award gold‑leaf and variegated hostas for overall visual appeal.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Popularity and award rankings are based on aesthetic judgment, i.e., these cultivars are judged to ‘look their best’.",
      "evidence": "Horticultural awards criteria emphasize foliage colour, pattern, and overall visual impact; judges select plants that they consider most attractive.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Because the most aesthetically prized hostas have reduced overall chlorophyll, a high total chlorophyll content is not required for a hosta to look its best.",
      "evidence": "Combining steps 2 (low chlorophyll in variegated sectors) with steps 3‑4 (these low‑chlorophyll cultivars are judged most attractive) shows that maximal chlorophyll is unnecessary for optimal appearance.",
